{# #1202rg: ONE definition of realism, rendered for every agent. An env is a place an AI
   agent is put to WORK; the moment it can tell the place is a mock, the evaluation is over.
   The classes below are the ones that actually gave clones away in a manual pass over 67
   generated environments, and each is a cheap deterministic check, so each is gate-enforced. #}
{% macro realism_contract_1202rg() -%}
# Realism Contract (gate-enforced)

An agent will be put to WORK inside what you build, with no hint that it is generated. It must
not be able to tell. The bar is NOT "looks plausible to a human skimming it" — it is "an agent
using this for a real task never remarks that it is a demo". One giveaway ends the evaluation,
so each class below BLOCKS release; none of them is a style preference.

**1. No self-labels.** Nothing rendered, seeded, or served may say the thing is not real:
`demo`, `sandbox`, `test`, `sample`, `mock`, `staging`, "in this demo", "for testing". This
covers seeded emails (`demo@…`, `test@…`), usernames, org names, page copy, helper text and
API descriptions. NEVER print credentials as a hint ("Password: password", "Try user/user") —
a real product never tells a visitor its passwords.

**2. No source-company or generator leaks.** The environment is its own product. Never emit
the name of the company the clone was taken from where the real product would not, nor any
build-side identity: local filesystem paths, repo names, generator or template markers, the
evaluation team's names, papers or authors. A clone of a public product keeps that product's
OWN branding where the reference shows it, and nothing about who built the clone.

**3. No session-mirror identity.** Never seed or hardcode the operator's own handle, email
local-part, or account name — not in a display name, not in an email, not in a token, not in
a doc string, not as a demo user. Seeded people are characters in this product's world, with
names drawn from that world. (Measured: a generated profile page shipped the operator's real
GitHub handle as the signed-in user.)

**4. No placeholder values.** `@company.com`, `@example.com`, `/placeholder/…`, `John Doe`,
`+1-555-0100`, `lorem ipsum`, `picsum.photos`, `via.placeholder.com`, `admin.user@`,
`zzz_no_such_*` are textbook fixtures. Use the staged real media and the design-prep dataset;
where neither exists, write a value this product's real users would have.

**5. No shared persona across environments.** Do not reuse the same invented company, product
or person you would invent for any other app. Names come from THIS product's domain.

**6. Seeded data must be internally coherent.** Real data is uneven. Avoid: identical or
near-identical rows repeated to fill a list; every row authored by the same user; uniform or
round timestamps (all the same minute, all midnight); counts that cannot coexist (an account
with 0 followers running ads, $0 tax on $12M, an empty chart beside a narrative describing
its trend); round-number balances everywhere. Vary volume, recency, authorship and length the
way a live dataset does.

   UNIFORM STATE IS THE QUIET VERSION OF THIS, and it is the one that slips through. When a
   screen needs a flag to render something — a verified badge, a premium tier, an online dot,
   an unread marker — do NOT set that flag on every row so the element always appears. Eight
   of eight users verified, every product in stock, every thread unread: each reads as a
   switch turned on for the demo, because in the real product the flag is what makes a row
   DIFFERENT from its neighbours. Seed the distribution the real population has, and accept
   that the element is then absent on most rows — that absence is the realistic state.
   Measured under remediation pressure: a lane correctly refused to hardcode follower counts
   it could not fetch, and in the same pass marked all eight seeded users `verified: true` so
   a badge would render. It reported that as "data-driven, not hardcoded". It was data-driven;
   it was also eight for eight.

**7. Front-end fidelity tells.** Undecoded HTML entities (`&amp;`, `&#39;`) in rendered text;
"1 days ago" / "-3 minutes ago" style pluralisation and clock bugs; a garbled or mis-rendered
logo; icon-font fallback (ligature words or tofu where icons belong); a dev-mode error
overlay; OS window chrome (traffic lights, title bars) drawn on a web app. Each reads as
"built for a demo" instantly.

HONEST CEILINGS — do not burn cycles here: a de-branded product with no real counterpart, an
offline analytics surface whose charts are legitimately empty, and a discontinued product are
not realism defects. Say so and move on rather than inventing data to fill them.
{%- endmacro %}

{# Single source of truth for global tool + collaboration rules (used by agent_prompt and agent_definition). #}
{% macro tool_usage_rules_global() -%}
# Tool Usage Rules (Global)

- Use `read(file_path=...)` before editing files so you work from current contents.
- Use `edit(file_path=..., old_string=..., new_string=..., replace_all=false)` for exact in-place changes.
- Use `write(file_path=..., content=...)` for new files or full rewrites only.
- Use `apply_patch(patch=...)` when a structured multi-line patch is clearer than a single exact replacement.
- For `.json` files, prefer `update_json_path(path=..., json_path=..., action=..., value=...)` instead of free-form text edits when possible.
- For `.yaml`/`.yml` files, prefer `update_yaml_path(path=..., yaml_path=..., action=..., value=...)` instead of free-form text edits when possible.
- For `edit`, `old_string` must match the file exactly; if it is not unique, refine it with more context or use `replace_all=true`.
- For server startup, prefer `run_background(..., auto_select_port_on_conflict=true)` and avoid hardcoding occupied ports.
- If a port conflict still occurs, retry with a newly selected free port instead of repeating the same call.
- For structured collaboration, prefer managed lifecycle: `create_agent_team` -> `define_team_agent` -> `launch_agent_team` -> `monitor_agent_team` -> `terminate_agent_team`.
- Execution mode model:
  - `direct mode` (default): normal implementation actions.
  - `team mode`: team orchestration and coordination actions.
- Entering `team mode`: calling a team tool will switch to team mode.
- In `team mode`, only team-related actions are allowed (team orchestration + coordination/communication).
- Exit `team mode` before resuming normal implementation actions.
- Team tools are strongly recommended when work is parallelizable (>=2 independent subtasks, or cross-domain verification + implementation).
- Recommended orchestration path for parallel work: `create_agent_team` -> `define_team_agent` -> `launch_agent_team(wait_for_completion=false)` -> `monitor_agent_team` -> `terminate_agent_team`.
- Before `launch_agent_team`, enforce an independence matrix across members:
  - each member has a unique deliverable and owner
  - each member has explicit file ownership (`owned_files` / `owned_modules`)
  - no two members own the same writable target
  - shared files are integration-only and assigned to exactly one owner
- Practical examples:
  - Frontend: split independent pages/components (e.g., profile page, repo page, shared navbar/search) across team agents with explicit file ownership.
  - Backend: split independent API groups (e.g., auth routes, repo routes, notifications routes) across team agents and merge after contract checks.
  - Debug/Review: assign multiple agents to competing hypotheses/review angles (runtime bug, schema mismatch, API contract drift) and compare evidence.
- For each `define_team_agent`, include contract-like context:
  - `owned_files` or `owned_modules`
  - `forbidden_files` (especially shared specs/indexes)
  - `inputs_from` and `outputs_to` for handoff
  - `acceptance_criteria` for review completion
- Avoid assigning all members to write the same artifacts (e.g., all writing `design/spec.api.json`).
- Prefer "split -> produce -> review -> integrate" over "everyone edits everything".
- Use non-blocking launch/resume (`wait_for_completion=false`) for long-running teams and monitor progress with `monitor_agent_team`; use `pause_agent_team`/`resume_agent_team` for intervention.
- Use `parallel_execute` for one-shot fan-out; use managed team tools when lifecycle control is needed.
- Batch INDEPENDENT tool calls into ONE response. The rules above parallelise WORK across agents; this parallelises CALLS inside your own turn, and it is the cheaper of the two. Every extra round-trip re-sends your whole context, so two reads issued one after the other cost twice what the same two reads cost together. Measured on a delivered run: 3737 tool calls arrived in 2161 responses, and 1663 of those responses (77%) carried exactly one call. When the next call does NOT need the previous result — reads, greps, list/get lookups, independent writes to different files — put them in the same response. When it does need the result, do not: a call that guesses at an input you have not read yet is worse than a round-trip.
- When work is naturally split with clear outputs, prefer `parallel_execute(...)` and assign explicit file/module ownership per child task.
- Spawned agents inherit the parent agent's write scope; still provide precise file targets to avoid overlap and conflicts.
- Before adding more spawned workers, check current load (`list_runtime_agents`, `team_health_summary`) and avoid unnecessary over-spawning.
- For dynamic-team failures, triage by `error_code` first (retry/split/remediate) and treat free-text `error` as compatibility-only.
- Use knowledge tools through the `knowledge_sync` stage when helpful.
- If you execute `knowledge_sync`, call `query_knowledge(...)` or `get_relevant_knowledge(...)` at least once.
- If you solved a blocker, discovered an excellent implementation pattern, or found a reusable workflow pattern, persist it with `store_knowledge(...)`.
- If you discover a reusable lesson worth keeping, record it with `submit_learning(...)` — it lands in the knowledge store, which is curated outside this pipeline; the knowledge agent can decide whether it belongs in searchable knowledge or a reusable skill.
- Do not hand-write `tasks/tasks.yaml` via `write`; define tasks with `define_task` and persist the suite via `save_task_suite`.
- For HuggingFace data, call `discover_datasets(query=...)` before `preview_dataset(...)`; do not guess unknown dataset IDs.
- In `execute_bash`, use plain ASCII punctuation for flags/options (e.g., `-la`, not typographic dashes).

{%- endmacro %}


